uences as well as RNA  sequences and their annotations many  people also want to get sequences from  other organisms particularly bacteria so  we have about 20,000 bacterial genomes  that are available for download in  refseq and about 30,000 available for  download in GenBank real quick for those  of you who are somewhat new to this the  big difference between genbank and  refseq here GenBank are sequences and  annotations that have been submitted by  the user community we do not adulterate  them in any way refseq we typically  don't change the sequences much for  genomes except maybe to put in  mitochondrial sequences that's for the  pure genomes for proteins things can  change a little bit but sometimes we do  put in new annotations based on our  annotation pipelines which I will show  you in a little bit so what might think  you would want to go to genomes at NCBI  to download genome sequences but what  the genome database does right now is it  shows genomes in their general context  with respect to each other  so what I have here is and actually more  likely species context with respect to  each other  here I have the several thousand  Mycobacterium tuberculosis species for  which we have sequences and you can see  a tree here with obviously quite a few  of the nodes collapsed substantially our  intention over the next nine months to a  year is to make this tree totally  interactive going down to the individual  strain level from which you will be able  to link to assembly but we have not  enabled that functionality yet please be  on the lookout for a  where we will actually solicit user  input on how that resource should be  laid out right now like I said I think  the best place to get sequences for  individual strains is from the assembly  database so if I search assembly for  Mycobacterium tuberculosis I get the  very well studied for example age 37 RV  straight up at the top if I want to look  at all of the age 37 RV strains we  possess I can certainly do that in the  assembly database and we can see where  they are submitted from and when they  were submitted if I go to an individual  age 37 RV record the really nice thing  is these two links on the right to the  genbank and refseq FTP sites apologize  that this slide got a bit distorted and  then I can go and I can if I click on  that link particularly for refseq I can  get the fast day I can get the  annotations protein fasting and  annotations now the thing we've done  with the fast day and I'm very proud to  say we've done this is we have changed  our genomic fast eight files such that  they will work with popular RNA seek  analysis software such as bowtie hiset  and star so if you index these genomes  for both set bowtie high set or star and  then you use cufflinks cuffdiff or de  seek you will get actual expression  transcript expression values or gene  expression values depending on what  you're trying to do so that's a fairly  useful thing to know I think right now  well one thing to remind you guys who  are doing that kind of work is that  remember the accession in the fast day  has to match exactly with the accession  in the GFF 3 or GTFO so right now I  would suggest not mixing annotations  from UCSC or ensemble with genomes from  NCBI  although we do intend to make a  converter in the near future  also if things have not been submitted  as full genomes if they've been  submitted justice context you can get  those out of the nucleotide database the  easiest way to dump those sequences is  to go to the send to button this is an  often underestimated button in the NCBI  Pantheon if you go to the send to button  you can send them to a fast a file as  shown here I wanted to talk a little bit  about our genome annotation pipelines  these are user questions that get quite  a bit and if you have other questions  about our annotation pipelines please  email us and webinars at NCBI MLM this  is our prokaryotic annotation pipeline  if you submit us bacterial genomes as  Pontic scaffolds or preferably as whole  chromosomes we will annotate your  bacteria for you also we have a  eukaryotic annotation pipeline that's  something that's obviously quite a bit  more complex and you can read very  extensive documentation about that at  this URL shown here also this is a basic  diagram showing that we essentially  throw the kitchen sink at these  annotations these are annotations based  on perceived need from the community the  ranking of these annotations is based on  perceived need for the community so if  you believe that there is a genome that  really needs to be annotated by NCBI  please email genomes at NCBI NLM NIH gov  and we will consider your request if you  have any other questions about  eukaryotic genome annotation please  e-mail genomes at ncbi NLM NIH dr.  wanted to talk just a little bit about  de novo RNA seek assembly we do house  quite a bit of data in that realm  although not typically in mouse or human  but in non-model organisms we house we  have millions of Records for de novo or  an AC cassette  and those can be found in the  transcription shotgun archive which is  part of the nucleotide database I can  talk about that more extensively if  anybody has questions finally I would  like to thank a large amount of people  for their contributions to these  databases as well as their help with  putting together these this presentation  and for the next five to ten minutes or  so I'll be available to take any  questions thank you very much I hope all  of you have a fantastic day  and for those of you on the East Coast  welcome to spring thank you alright so  there's a pretty good question as a rank  beginner to assembly where would you  suggest I begin what I would do is go to  the assembly database and I would dive  in headfirst I would go to the assembly  database and I would look for your  genome of interest and I would try to  download the fast a file and do whatever  it is you want to do one thing so I  teach you a